WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:21.567
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. Architectures and design patterns of microservices.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.808
A pattern is a named, reusable answer to a recurring problem.

3
00:00:37.188 --> 00:00:39.324
Circuit breaker.

4
00:00:40.364 --> 00:00:41.200
Saga.

5
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.657
API gateway.

6
00:00:44.697 --> 00:00:50.107
Microservice architecture is one style; the patterns are the decisions inside that style.

7
00:00:50.487 --> 00:01:05.115
Chris Richardson’s pattern language at microservices.io is the catalog architects actually use — he dates it to the 18th of March 2014, a week before the Lewis and Fowler article, and published it as ‘Microservices Patterns’ in 2018.

8
00:01:06.095 --> 00:01:11.308
An application is a set of services that can be deployed on their own and stay loosely coupled.

9
00:01:11.688 --> 00:01:15.636
Richardson keeps the monolith in the language too, as the other style.

10
00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:28.694
Moving from one to the other is itself a design: the strangler application — Fowler’s metaphor of the fig that grows around its host tree — plus an anti-corruption layer, so the new model is translated at the edge of the old one.

11
00:01:29.674 --> 00:01:31.183
Where is the boundary?

12
00:01:32.223 --> 00:01:37.401
Decompose by business capability, following Lewis and Fowler.

13
00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:43.294
Decompose by subdomain, following Eric Evans’s bounded contexts.

14
00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:52.043
Later additions: service per team, and the self-contained service — one that answers a synchronous request without waiting on anyone else.

15
00:01:52.423 --> 00:01:55.175
Boundary size is the hard judgment.

16
00:01:56.215 --> 00:02:00.882
Forces that split a system pull against forces that hold it together.

17
00:02:01.862 --> 00:02:05.693
Database per service is the data architecture that makes the rest necessary.

18
00:02:06.073 --> 00:02:10.682
Each service has a private database, so a schema change stays inside one team.

19
00:02:11.062 --> 00:02:15.555
Once data is private, one business action is no longer one database transaction.

20
00:02:16.255 --> 00:02:20.249
Everything in the next two minutes follows from that sentence.

21
00:02:21.229 --> 00:02:24.747
A saga is a sequence of local transactions.

22
00:02:25.787 --> 00:02:29.398
If a later step fails, earlier steps run compensations.

23
00:02:29.778 --> 00:02:36.314
The idea is from Garcia-Molina and Salem, 1987; Richardson applied it to services.

24
00:02:37.014 --> 00:02:38.988
It has two structures.

25
00:02:40.028 --> 00:02:49.432
Choreography: the order service publishes OrderPlaced, inventory and payment react and publish their own events, and no coordinator holds the whole story.

26
00:02:49.812 --> 00:02:56.697
Orchestration: one coordinator tells inventory to reserve, then payment to charge, and runs the compensation if payment fails.

27
00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.859
Queries have their own patterns.

28
00:03:00.899 --> 00:03:05.300
API composition asks each owner and joins the answers in memory.

29
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.048
CQRS keeps a separate read model.

30
00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:16.240
A command-side replica gives a service a local, read-only copy of data it does not own.

31
00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:24.397
And publishing events safely is its own group — transactional outbox, transaction log tailing, polling publisher.

32
00:03:24.777 --> 00:03:29.548
The point of all three is that the database write and the message leave together.

33
00:03:30.528 --> 00:03:35.776
Remote procedure invocation — HTTP, gRPC — is the synchronous pipe.

34
00:03:36.156 --> 00:03:42.576
Messaging is the asynchronous one, and consumers are designed to be idempotent because a message can arrive twice.

35
00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:45.731
Outside clients meet an API gateway, one front door.

36
00:03:46.431 --> 00:03:53.815
A backend for frontend is a gateway per client experience — web, iOS, Android — owned by the team that ships that client.

37
00:03:54.195 --> 00:03:57.167
The rule that makes it work: one experience, one backend.

38
00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:01.793
Callers find instances through a service registry.

39
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:10.704
A circuit breaker — Michael Nygard’s name, from ‘Release It!’ in 2007 — stops calling a dependency whose failures have crossed a threshold.

40
00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:14.451
Timeouts, retries, and bulkheads travel with it.

41
00:04:15.491 --> 00:04:18.626
Identity travels as an access token.

42
00:04:19.666 --> 00:04:30.010
And after the call, the system is understood through log aggregation, metrics, distributed tracing, and a health check API — because a single request is now a path across processes.

43
00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:41.718
Deployment patterns choose the unit that starts and scales — one service instance per container became the ordinary choice after Docker and Kubernetes; serverless is the other extreme.

44
00:04:42.098 --> 00:04:55.775
Shared mechanics sit in a microservice chassis, a framework every service uses, or, from about 2016, in a sidecar and a service mesh — a proxy beside the service that carries retries, discovery, and traffic policy.

45
00:04:56.755 --> 00:05:01.004
Richardson groups the whole language by the question an architect is answering.

46
00:05:01.384 --> 00:05:03.509
Sixteen questions.

47
00:05:04.549 --> 00:05:07.010
Where is the boundary.

48
00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:10.116
Who owns the data.

49
00:05:11.156 --> 00:05:13.711
How does a command cross services.

50
00:05:14.751 --> 00:05:16.213
How does a query.

51
00:05:17.253 --> 00:05:18.937
How is change recorded.

52
00:05:19.977 --> 00:05:22.612
How is a message published with the write.

53
00:05:23.652 --> 00:05:25.510
How do services talk.

54
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.350
How do clients enter.

55
00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:31.305
How is an instance found.

56
00:05:32.345 --> 00:05:34.772
How does failure stay local.

57
00:05:35.812 --> 00:05:37.855
How does identity travel.

58
00:05:38.895 --> 00:05:40.718
How is the system seen.

59
00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:43.058
How is it deployed.

60
00:05:44.098 --> 00:05:45.828
How is a screen built.

61
00:05:46.868 --> 00:05:48.877
How do you leave a monolith.

62
00:05:49.917 --> 00:05:52.308
Where do shared mechanics live.

63
00:05:53.628 --> 00:05:56.194
These names are the before-state.

64
00:05:57.234 --> 00:06:12.443
A later claim that AI influenced microservice architecture has to name a decision in this sequence: where boundaries are drawn, how a saga is coordinated, whether the pipe stays dumb, or whether the sidecar and the mesh absorb the reliability patterns.

65
00:06:13.423 --> 00:06:15.374
Three things to carry away.

66
00:06:16.414 --> 00:06:17.040
One.

67
00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.121
Patterns are decisions, arranged as the questions an architect meets in order.

68
00:06:22.821 --> 00:06:23.343
Two.

69
00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:29.759
Private data makes everything else necessary: sagas, composition, CQRS, outboxes.

70
00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:30.847
Three.

71
00:06:31.887 --> 00:06:35.381
Any AI claim must name one decision in this sequence.

72
00:06:36.701 --> 00:06:43.075
Next: the whole catalog on one wall — fifteen jobs, sixty-one patterns, and where each name comes from.
